          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>According to the narration, which pair of defects acting together forms the esse</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>According to the narration, which pair of defects acting together forms the essential core of Type 2 diabetes mellitus?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Autoimmune destruction of beta cells and absolute insulin deficiency</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This is the mechanism of Type 1 diabetes, which follows HLA-DR3/DR4-linked autoimmunity and is ketosis-prone. It is not the core of Type 2.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Peripheral insulin resistance and progressive beta-cell failure</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Neither defect alone causes Type 2 diabetes. Resistance is initially compensated by hyperinsulinaemia, and hyperglycaemia becomes overt only when beta-cell function fails.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Increased hepatic glucose output and reduced incretin effect</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Both are part of the ominous octet, but they are two of the eight converging mechanisms. The essential core is the twin defect of insulin resistance and beta-cell failure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Loss of GLUT-4 transporters and islet autoantibody production</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Islet autoantibodies such as anti-GAD65 mark Type 1 diabetes. Type 2 is not autoimmune, and the essential core of Type 2 is insulin resistance with beta-cell failure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Type 2 diabetes requires two defects acting together: peripheral insulin resistance and progressive beta-cell failure. Autoimmune beta-cell destruction defines Type 1 diabetes."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Right. Type 2 needs insulin resistance and progressive beta-cell failure together. That is why it is gradual and initially compensated, unlike the abrupt, ketosis-prone Type 1."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
