        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 366.425,
            "to": 376.425
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 58-year-old man with 15 years of diabetes has a swollen, warm, deformed midfoo</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 58-year-old man with 15 years of diabetes has a swollen, warm, deformed midfoot. It is painless, the skin is intact, and the dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are palpable. He has no fever. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Charcot neuroarthropathy</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. A warm, painless, deformed foot with palpable pulses in a neuropathic diabetic patient is Charcot neuroarthropathy. Loss of protective sensation allows repeated microtrauma and joint destruction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Diabetic foot infection with cellulitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Infection usually brings pain or tenderness, a portal of entry such as an ulcer, erythema and often systemic features. Treating Charcot as infection changes management wrongly.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Critical limb ischaemia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>An ischaemic foot is cold and pulseless with rest pain. Here the foot is warm and the pulses are palpable.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Acute gouty arthritis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Acute gout is intensely painful, and a painless deformed warm foot in long-standing diabetic neuropathy points to Charcot.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key combination is a warm, painless, deformed foot with palpable pulses. That is Charcot neuroarthropathy, not infection or ischaemia, and it changes management entirely."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Yes. Warm, painless, deformed and with palpable pulses means Charcot. Confirm neuropathy with the 10-gram monofilament and manage with offloading and immobilisation."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
