        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 591.15,
            "to": 601.15
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 60-year-old man with Type 2 diabetes has an eGFR of 55 and macroalbuminuria. H</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 60-year-old man with Type 2 diabetes has an eGFR of 55 and macroalbuminuria. His HbA1c is 8.4% on metformin alone, and he has no established cardiovascular disease. Which drug should be added to lifestyle measures and his ACE inhibitor for renoprotection?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Glibenclamide</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A sulphonylurea lowers glucose but causes hypoglycaemia and weight gain. It offers no renoprotection, and prolonged hypoglycaemia is a particular risk with reduced renal function.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pioglitazone</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pioglitazone improves insulin sensitivity but causes fluid retention and carries heart failure risk. It is not chosen for kidney protection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A DPP-4 inhibitor</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>DPP-4 inhibitors are weight-neutral and kidney-safe, but they have no proven renoprotective benefit.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>An SGLT2 inhibitor</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. SGLT2 inhibitors lower blood pressure and protect the kidneys down to an eGFR of about 20, largely independent of glucose control. Monitor potassium and volume status when combining with an ACE inhibitor.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Follow the treat-to-benefit principle. For albuminuric kidney disease, choose an SGLT2 inhibitor for renoprotection alongside an ACE inhibitor or ARB."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Yes. Comorbidity, not HbA1c alone, drives the choice. Kidney disease points to an SGLT2 inhibitor, and established cardiovascular disease points to a GLP-1 receptor agonist."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
