        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 731.05,
            "to": 741.05
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A teenager with DKA has a glucose of 420 mg/dL and a pH of 7.18. IV 0.9% saline </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A teenager with DKA has a glucose of 420 mg/dL and a pH of 7.18. IV 0.9% saline has been started. His serum potassium result is 3.1 mmol/L. What is the most appropriate next step?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Start insulin infusion at 0.1 units/kg/hour immediately</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Insulin drives potassium into cells. Starting it with a potassium below 3.5 mmol/L can precipitate fatal hypokalaemia and arrhythmia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Give 25 mL of 50% dextrose intravenously</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Dextrose is added to fluids when glucose falls to about 200 mg/dL. Here glucose is 420 mg/dL, so it is not indicated.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hold insulin and replace potassium until it is above 3.5 mmol/L</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Total body potassium is depleted in DKA. Correct it first, then start insulin, and continue insulin until ketones clear, not just until glucose normalises.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Give sodium bicarbonate to correct the pH before anything else</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fluids and insulin correct the acidosis in DKA. Bicarbonate is not routine, and it does not address the low potassium.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In DKA, always check potassium before insulin. If it is below 3.5 mmol/L, hold insulin and replace potassium first, because insulin shifts potassium into cells and can cause a fatal drop."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Right. Fluids come first, then check potassium. If it is below 3.5 mmol/L, hold insulin and replace potassium first."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
